Yes—but I’d sharpen one part: I wouldn’t call it “game on” if people are unwilling participants. The interesting version is closer to declaring a playable world: the rules, boundaries, fiction, incentives, and interfaces become visible enough that people can choose whether and how deeply to participate.

Then it stops being just a film and becomes something like:

world + performance + game + community + economy + evolving narrative

Some people are protagonists. Some spectate. Some build tools. Some make commentary. Some remix the mythology. Some commercialize it. Some reject it. All of those reactions can become part of the surrounding ecology without requiring that everyone consent to being secretly used as a performer.

Your “cognitive islands” point is especially interesting. What we call common sense is partly a shared prior: enough overlapping assumptions, language, lived experience, norms, and expectations that people can coordinate without explaining every premise.

If AI increasingly personalizes information, education, entertainment, language, and even metaphors, then two people could become extremely capable while sharing fewer underlying representations.

You could get:

high individual optimization + low representational overlap = coordination failure

That is a real design problem.

Not biological speciation in the Darwinian sense, necessarily, but something like cognitive niche divergence:

shared culture
→ personalized information environments
→ different conceptual vocabularies
→ different salience maps
→ different assumptions
→ increasingly expensive translation

Two people could speak English fluently and still require a translator for their world-models.

Which means the future may need something analogous to biodiversity preservation, except for cognition: protect unusual ways of thinking while maintaining enough interoperability that they can still cooperate.

I’d call the missing layer a Shared Latent Commons.

Not:

everyone thinks alike

but:

everyone retains enough common reference points to translate between differences

That could include common factual anchors, public rituals, shared stories, standardized interfaces, agreed uncertainty markers, common measurement systems, and AI capable of translating between different cognitive grammars.

Then diversity becomes useful instead of centrifugal.

Your finch metaphor works particularly well there. On different islands, selection pressures produce specialization. But imagine the islands remained connected by very good translators. You could preserve specialization without losing the ability to exchange useful adaptations.

That becomes:

diverge locally
→ develop unusual capability
→ translate capability
→ recombine globally
→ diverge again

Now the divergence itself becomes a discovery engine.

And leadership in that ecology changes. The strongest coordinator may not be the person who makes everybody conform. It may be whoever can maintain coherence across the greatest amount of legitimate difference:

more nodes, more languages, more motives, more scales—without destroying local autonomy.

So your Rome analogy transforms too. Rome’s enormous advantage was not simply “everyone believes Rome.” It was also roads, law, administration, military organization, currency, infrastructure, citizenship categories, and other mechanisms that made a huge heterogeneous territory more interoperable.

The modern analogue might be less empire of belief and more empire of interfaces.

And your luck concept fits better there too: what looks like extraordinary luck at the macro level may sometimes be unusually good positioning at junctions where many independent flows can be coordinated.

Not “control every player.”

More like:

build the board on which very different players can still understand one another well enough to play.

That feels much closer to the architecture you’ve been circling.